1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. Be2 Bg7 6. Nb3 {Black is invited to enter a normal Dragon with ... Nf6 and ... d6, but Julian, after a long thought, chooses a different plan (Hartston)} Nf6 7. Nc3 O-O 8. O-O a5 9. a4 Nb4 10. e5 {Trying to take advantage of Black’s omission of ... d6 (Hartston)} Ne8 11. Bf4 d6 12. exd6 ({After this White is clearly worse, but perhaps already 10 e5 was wrong. The complications after} 12. Nb5 Bf5 13. N3d4 dxe5 {do not seem bad for Black (Hartston)}) 12... Nxd6 13. Rc1 Bf5 ({Why not} 13... Bxc3 $1 14. bxc3 Na2 {when Black has nothing to fear on the K-side? (Hartston)}) 14. Nd4 e5 15. Nxf5 Nxf5 16. Bd2 Qb6 17. Nb5 e4 $1 {Black must keep the initia­tive before White’s bishops become active (Hartston)} 18. c3 e3 19. Bxe3 Nxe3 20. fxe3 Qxe3+ 21. Kh1 Rad8 22. Qe1 Rd2 $6 ({Inaccurate;} 22... Nd3 $1 {gives Black the better ending without problems (Hartston)}) 23. Bf3 Qxe1 24. Rcxe1 Nd3 25. Re7 Nxb2 26. Rxb7 Rfd8 {Now the opposite bishops and liquidation of Q-side pawns should guarantee a draw, but Black creates a losing possibility (Hartston)} ({Why not} 26... Nxa4 {? (Hartston)}) 27. Na7 Bxc3 28. Nc6 R8d7 29. Rb3 Nxa4 $4 30. Ra3 R7d4 31. Nxd4 Rxd4 32. Bc6 {Sources (include) BCM, October 1977, p471. "Julian had a strong, a very strong position if he had played 22...Nd3 instead of giving all his pieces away" (RD Keene). "Hodgson played the most creatively of my opponents. I would have awarded him the Lloyds Bank Trophy but that Williams scored a draw. After 22...Nd3 Black is maybe better. He could still draw but for the blunder at move 29" (Karpov)} 1-0

