1. e4 c5 2. c3 e6 3. d4 d5 4. e5 Qb6 5. Nf3 Bd7 6. Be2 Bb5 7. c4 Bxc4 8. Bxc4 Qb4+ 9. Nbd2 dxc4 10. O-O cxd4 11. Nxd4 Nd7 12. Qe2 Rc8 13. N2f3 Ne7 14. a3 Qa4 15. Bd2 Nc6 16. Rac1 Nb6 17. Be3 Be7 18. Rc2 O-O 19. Rfc1 Na5 20. Ng5 Nd5 21. Bd2 Bxg5 22. Bxg5 b5 23. Qg4 Kh8 24. Qg3 $2 (24. Rc3 $1 Nxc3 25. Rxc3 {intending Rh3 and Qh5 should be at least enough to draw - JE Littlewood}) 24... Nc6 25. Nxc6 Rxc6 26. Re2 c3 27. Ree1 Rfc8 28. bxc3 Rxc3 29. Rxc3 Rxc3 30. Be3 Qxa3 31. Qh4 Rc8 32. h3 a5 {"Igor Zaitsev used to play this 7 c4 gambit in the late 1960s, but none of his opponents played the move 8...Qb4+ which I analysed at that time. I could not remember the analysis exactly but my opponent made some inaccuracies. After 24 Rc3 Nxc3 25 Rxc3 Black can play f6, f5 or even Nb3 and I don't believe the sacrifice is correct. This was an interesting game." (Karpov)} 0-1

