1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. c3 Nf6 4. e5 Nd5 5. d4 cxd4 6. cxd4 b6 7. Nc3 Bb7 8. Bd3 Be7 9. O-O O-O 10. Re1 d6 11. exd6 Qxd6 12. Ne4 Qd8 13. Bg5 Nc6 14. Bb1 Re8 15. a3 Rc8 16. Qd3 g6 17. Bxe7 Rxe7 18. Ba2 Kg7 19. Rad1 f6 20. h4 Re8 21. Qd2 Nce7 22. Ng3 Nf5 23. Nxf5+ gxf5 24. Rc1 Qd6 25. Qe2 Rxc1 26. Rxc1 Kh8 27. Bxd5 Bxd5 28. Rc3 Rg8 29. Ne1 Qf4 30. Qh5 Qe4 31. Nf3 Qg4 32. Qxg4 Rxg4 33. Kf1 h5 34. Rc8+ Kg7 35. Rc7+ Kg6 36. Rxa7 Bc4+ 37. Kg1 Be2 38. Kh2 Bxf3 39. gxf3 Rxh4+ 40. Kg2 Rxd4 41. Rb7 Rd6 42. a4 f4 43. b4 e5 44. a5 bxa5 45. bxa5 Rd3 46. a6 Ra3 47. a7 f5 48. Re7 Kf6 49. Rh7 e4 50. fxe4 fxe4 51. Rxh5 Rxa7 52. Rb5 Ra2 53. Kf1 f3 54. Ke1 Re2+ 55. Kf1 Ke6 56. Ra5 Kd6 57. Rb5 Rc2 58. Ke1 $2 (58. Kg1) 58... Rc5 59. Rb8 Ke5 60. Rd8 Rc1+ 61. Kd2 Rf1 62. Re8+ Kf4 63. Rf8+ Kg4 64. Rg8+ Kh3 65. Ke3 Re1+ 66. Kf4 Kh2 67. Rg7 Rg1 68. Rh7+ Kg2 69. Kxe4 Kxf2 70. Ra7 Rg4+ 71. Kd3 Rg1 72. Ra8 Kg2 {"White played well and had an even position well into the middlegame. In the ending he made a fundamental mistake with Ke1 instead of Kg1. The king must stay on the short side of the black pawn so that the white rook can operate on the long side after Black takes on f2. This is another case of the importance of studying endgames" (Karpov)} 0-1

