1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be2 Bg7 7. O-O Nc6 8. Nb3 O-O 9. Bg5 Be6 10. Kh1 Na5 11. f4 a6 12. f5 (12. Qe1) 12... Bc4 13. Nxa5 Bxe2 14. Qxe2 Qxa5 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Nd5 Qd8 17. c3 Rc8 18. Rad1 Bg7 19. Qg4 Re8 20. Qf3 Rf8 21. Qh3 Rc4 22. Rde1 Re8 23. Qf3 Rf8 24. Ne3 Rc5 25. Ng4 Qd7 26. Qh3 Qc8 27. Rf3 Qe8 28. Ref1 Rc8 29. Nh6+ Kh8 30. fxg6 fxg6 31. Nf7+ Kg8 32. Qe6 {"One of my best games of the match, although Black got into time trouble" (Karpov). Webb had studied Gligoric's article on the game Karpov-Sosonko at Bad Lauterberg, made available to him with later games from other tournaments in special secret preparation by the England team trainers. He hoped to follow the game Adorjan-Sosonko at the IBM tournament, where the inaccurate 12 Qe1 gave Black satisfactory play. After the game, however, Webb's conclusion was that he should start playing 9 Bg5 with the white pieces. "It's a good and interesting system" (Karpov)} 1-0

